  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Subtropical Storm Josephine, located approx 400\nNE of Cape Hatteras, will begin to accelerate northeastward \ntoday as a northern stream shortwave digs into the Northeast \nCONUS. While winds will continue to diminish across the region \ntoday, large surf will continue to bring coastal flooding \nimpacts along vulnerable locations of the coast, especially \nalong Hatteras Island where dune structure is fragile. The \nNational Parks Service and NCDOT have reported substantial beach\nerosion from south Nags Head to Buxton, along with significant \noverwash occurring on side streets in Rodanthe. Due to hazardous\nroad conditions and flooding, NC-12 remains closed from the \nMarc Basnight Bridge to Rodanthe. The next high tides are around\n5 AM and 5 PM today. A Coastal Flood Warning remains in effect \nfor Hatteras Island and a Coastal Flood Advisory for the \nNorthern Outer Banks. Have cancelled the Coastal Flood Advisory \nfor Ocracoke Island. Additionally, a high surf advisory \ncontinues for the beaches from Duck to Cape Hatteras where surf \nheights 8-12 ft will continue today. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pleasantly cooler air mass will remain with us\nthrough the next several days with temps a bit below climo. \nMaximum temperatures through the period are expected to remain \nin the mid 60s to lower 70s range. Overnight lows will range \nfrom the 40s inland to the 50s coast. Good radiational cooling\nconditions early this morning with clear skies and calm winds \ninland from the coast have allowed the coldest locations to drop\ninto the upper 30s. Could see a few upper 30s again tonight with\nclear skies and calm winds expected. A bit more mixing ensues\nFriday night as strong high pressure builds in from the north\npreventing temps from cratering. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Strong high pressure building in from the north\nand low pressure well off the SE coast will result in a period\nof breezy to strong NE winds returning late Friday through the\nweekend with strongest winds along the coast, especially south\nof Cape Hatteras where gradients will be tightest. This, \ncombined with developing King Tides could lead to additional \nminor coastal impacts (coastal flooding and rough surf).",
